  "text": "This work gives a thorough yet accessible overview of how long‑wavelength light is used to study the behavior of solid materials. It is aimed at researchers and senior students in physics, chemistry, and related areas who need a single source that links the basic ideas with practical uses. The book starts with fundamental concepts, then moves on to how molecules absorb this radiation, followed by a discussion of how the crystal lattice and electronic structures of non‑metal solids respond. It also describes the operation of detectors, the design of instruments that separate wavelengths, and the role of filters, as well as the special considerations for the far‑infrared region. Practical examples of how these methods are applied are included, together with appendices that list useful equations in common unit systems and a brief note on advanced quantum‑mechanical calculations."
